// Keyboard remapping that is not available as a stock xkb option.
//
// Reached by name from .config/xkb/rules/evdev, which is what turns the section
// below into the option "custom:capslock_shift_f12" that
// .config/niri/config.kdl names. libxkbcommon 1.13.1 searches
// $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xkb ahead of /usr/share/X11/xkb, so this file is found
// without anything being installed outside $HOME.

// Caps Lock is taken by Control -- the stock option ctrl:nocaps does that half
// -- so the Caps Lock function moves here, onto Shift+F12.
//
// One group, deliberately. A key defines its own number of groups, and xkb
// resolves an effective group beyond that back into the key's own range, so a
// single-group definition answers in every layout. Writing symbols[Group1] and
// symbols[Group2] explicitly would mean a new stanza for every layout ever
// added, and the remap is meant to hold whatever the layout is: layout is a
// per-keyboard preference and this is not.
//
// F12 stays on the first level, so unshifted F12 is untouched. The keysym does
// the rest: compat/complete carries `augment "caps(caps_lock)"`, whose
// interpret binds a Caps_Lock keysym to LockMods(modifiers = Lock) wherever it
// sits. Its own comment says that exists precisely so Caps_Lock can be used off
// the first level, which is this.
partial modifier_keys
xkb_symbols "capslock_shift_f12" {
    replace key <FK12> { [ F12, Caps_Lock ] };
};
